# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-ccl-parity-missing-load-file-message -- under alfe-ccl the two clautolisp variants word a missing -l file differently
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: none
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-ccl-parity-missing-load-file-message

* Symptom

=make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl= fails one check (found 2026-10-06 while
working on debugger-public-interface-and-on-error; untouched by that work):
the parity row "missing load file" of
CLAUTOLISP-BACKEND-VARIANTS-ARE-SEMANTICALLY-IDENTICAL.

#+begin_example
direct:     clautolisp: No such file or directory : "/nonexistent/parity-missing.lsp"
subprocess: clautolisp: The file #P"/nonexistent/parity-missing.lsp" does not exist:
              No such file or directory
#+end_example

* Repro

#+begin_src sh
make -C clautolisp build-clautolisp-sbcl
make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl
#+end_src

* Cause

REPORT-ENGINE-ERROR prints the host Lisp's FILE-ERROR report. Under alfe-ccl
the in-process engine runs on CCL, the subprocess child is the SBCL-built
clautolisp-sbcl, and the two implementations word the condition differently.
The SBCL lane passes because both sides are SBCL. Fix: have the engine report a
missing load file in its own words (pathname + strerror), independent of the
host Lisp's FILE-ERROR report.

* Resolution (clautolisp 2.2.217 / alfe 2.2.161, 2026-10-06)

REPORT-ENGINE-ERROR has a FILE-ERROR branch: FILE-ERROR-MESSAGE words it
=PROGRAM: cannot open FILE: REASON=, REASON being what the file system says
now (=no such file or directory=, or =permission denied= for a file that is
there), the same text on every host Lisp. Both variants print it, so the
parity row agrees under alfe-ccl too. Tests: the clautolisp exit-status test
asserts the message; the actions-load-missing-file scenario asserts it on
stderr.

The same lane then showed a second CCL divergence, row "load file not in the
source encoding" (from sysexits-exit-statuses): CCL's file streams REPLACE an
invalid UTF-8 sequence with U+FFFD instead of signalling, so the in-process
engine loaded a latin-1 file (exit 0) that clautolisp-sbcl refuses (EX_DATAERR
65). The reader now checks a host-decoded source: a U+FFFD the file does not
literally contain (EF BF BD) signals CLAUTOLISP.AUTOLISP-READER:
SOURCE-DECODING-ERROR, reported and mapped exactly like the host's decoding
error. Test: READ-FILE-WITH-INVALID-UTF-8-SIGNALS-ON-EVERY-HOST (both lanes).
